      "text": "We start with the given equation of the circle:\n\\[x^2 - 6x + y^2 + 2y + 6 = 0.\\]\n\nFirst, we complete the square for both the \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) terms.\n\nStarting with the \\(x\\) terms:\n\\[x^2 - 6x.\\]\nTo complete the square, we take half of the coefficient of \\(x\\), which is \\(-6\\), divide by 2 to get \\(-3\\), and then square it to get \\(9\\). So, we can write:\n\\[x^2 - 6x = (x - 3)^2 - 9.\\]\n\nNext, for the \\(y\\) terms:\n\\[y^2 + 2y.\\]\nTo complete the square, we take half of the coefficient of \\(y\\), which is \\(2\\), divide by 2 to get \\(1\\), and then square it to get \\(1\\). So, we can write:\n\\[y^2 + 2y = (y + 1)^2 - 1.\\]\n\nNow, we substitute these completed squares back into the original equation:\n\\[(x - 3)^2 - 9 + (y + 1)^2 - 1 + 6 = 0.\\]\n\nSimplifying the constants on the left side:\n\\[(x - 3)^2 + (y + 1)^2 - 4 = 0,\\]\n\\[(x - 3)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 4.\\]\n\nThis is the standard form of the equation of a circle \\((x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2 = r^2\\), where \\((h, k)\\) is the center and \\(r\\) is the radius. From the equation \\((x - 3)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 4\\), we can see that the center of the circle is \\((3, -1)\\) and the radius \\(r\\) is \\(\\sqrt{4} = 2\\).\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
