      "text": "To find the radius of the circle given by the equation \\(x^2 - 6x + y^2 + 2y + 6 = 0\\), we need to rewrite the equation in the standard form of a circle's equation, \\((x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2 = r^2\\), where \\((h, k)\\) is the center and \\(r\\) is the radius.\n\nWe will do this by completing the square for both the \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) terms.\n\n1. Start with the given equation:\n   \\[\n   x^2 - 6x + y^2 + 2y + 6 = 0\n   \\]\n\n2. Rearrange the equation to group the \\(x\\) terms and the \\(y\\) terms together:\n   \\[\n   x^2 - 6x + y^2 + 2y = -6\n   \\]\n\n3. Complete the square for the \\(x\\) terms:\n   - Take the coefficient of \\(x\\), which is \\(-6\\), divide it by 2 to get \\(-3\\), and then square it to get \\(9\\).\n   - Add and subtract \\(9\\) inside the equation:\n     \\[\n     x^2 - 6x + 9 - 9\n     \\]\n   - This can be written as:\n     \\[\n     (x - 3)^2 - 9\n     \\]\n\n4. Complete the square for the \\(y\\) terms:\n   - Take the coefficient of \\(y\\), which is \\(2\\), divide it by 2 to get \\(1\\), and then square it to get \\(1\\).\n   - Add and subtract \\(1\\) inside the equation:\n     \\[\n     y^2 + 2y + 1 - 1\n     \\]\n   - This can be written as:\n     \\[\n     (y + 1)^2 - 1\n     \\]\n\n5. Substitute these completed squares back into the equation:\n   \\[\n   (x - 3)^2 - 9 + (y + 1)^2 - 1 = -6\n   \\]\n\n6. Simplify the equation:\n   \\[\n   (x - 3)^2 + (y + 1)^2 - 10 = -6\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   (x - 3)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 4\n   \\]\n\nNow, the equation is in the standard form \\((x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2 = r^2\\), where \\((h, k) = (3, -1)\\) and \\(r^2 = 4\\).\n\nTherefore, the radius \\(r\\) is:\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{4} = 2\n\\]\n\nThe radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
