    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the radius of the circle given by the equation \\(x^2 - 6x + y^2 + 2y + 6 = 0\\), we need to rewrite the equation in the standard form \\((x-h)^2 + (y-k)^2 = r^2\\). This involves completing the square for both the \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) terms.\n\n1. Start by focusing on the \\(x\\) terms: \\(x^2 - 6x\\).\n   - To complete the square, take the coefficient of \\(x\\), which is \\(-6\\), divide it by 2 to get \\(-3\\), and then square it to get \\(9\\).\n   - Add and subtract \\(9\\) inside the equation:\n     \\[\n     x^2 - 6x = (x-3)^2 - 9\n     \\]\n\n2. Next, focus on the \\(y\\) terms: \\(y^2 + 2y\\).\n   - To complete the square, take the coefficient of \\(y\\), which is \\(2\\), divide it by 2 to get \\(1\\), and then square it to get \\(1\\).\n   - Add and subtract \\(1\\) inside the equation:\n     \\[\n     y^2 + 2y = (y+1)^2 - 1\n     \\]\n\n3. Substitute these completed squares back into the original equation:\n   \\[\n   x^2 - 6x + y^2 + 2y + 6 = 0\n   \\]\n   becomes\n   \\[\n   (x-3)^2 - 9 + (y+1)^2 - 1 + 6 = 0\n   \\]\n\n4. Simplify the equation:\n   \\[\n   (x-3)^2 + (y+1)^2 - 4 = 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   (x-3)^2 + (y+1)^2 = 4\n   \\]\n\nNow, the equation is in the standard form \\((x-h)^2 + (y-k)^2 = r^2\\), where \\((h, k)\\) is the center and \\(r\\) is the radius. Here, the center \\((h, k)\\) is \\((3, -1)\\) and the radius \\(r\\) is \\(\\sqrt{4} = 2\\).\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
